{
 "id": "aero",
 "name": "Aerodynamics",
 "summary": "Downforce and drag management for the Gen 6 Camaro: factory ZL1 1LE aero (larger splitter, dive planes, exposed-carbon rear wing, carbon hood vent) as the baseline, or chassis-mounted race splitter, canards, undertray, adjustable wing and diffuser where class rules allow.",
 "overview": "The ZL1 1LE package adds a larger unique splitter, front dive planes and a tall exposed carbon rear wing (RPO T42) to the ZL1; Chevrolet credited it with a 3-second gain around its Milford road course, and one press source quotes ~300 lb of rear downforce at top speed. For racing, the critical difference is load paths: the splitter must be carried by brackets to the crash beam/subframe and adjustable rods, not by the plastic fascia; the wing should load through uprights into mount plates and preferably chassis structure, not the decklid skin.\n\nBalance is tuned with wing angle and Gurney height at the rear against splitter height/extension and canards at the front. Underbody flow is cleaned up with a front undertray (also protecting the oil pan), optional flat mid-floor, rocker-skirt blades and a rear diffuser. Hood extractor and fender louvers reduce front lift and help cooling.\n\nCheck every item against the class rules (NASA ST/GTS, SCCA, Trans Am) – splitter overhang, wing span/height and flat bottoms are commonly limited.",
 "buildNotes": [
  "Set ride height/rake first.",
  "Mount splitter to chassis brackets, then rods, then fascia edge fasteners.",
  "Fit undertray and side-skirt blades.",
  "Mount wing plates/chassis struts before decklid paint; install uprights, element, endplates, Gurney.",
  "Baseline AoA mid-range; balance during shakedown."
 ],
